{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788191455,"nonce":"XtMRU+OQVUK6lTujgKdNceJlH7g=","message":"BUILT, NOT VERIFIED. Branch `spec/orders-makingfor`, 4 commits, clean\n`-c Release` (0 warnings). All four consequences in the body confirmed by member.\n\nONE ATTRIBUTION I GOT WRONG: the \"missing 30 steel\" string is in\n`RimWorld/WorkGiver_ConstructDeliverResources.cs` `JobOnThing`, not\n`WorkGiver_DoBill`. And it is worse than the body said — without `makingFor`\nthat method also `break`s at the FIRST unavailable resource instead of\ncollecting them all, so the fix widens the reason as well as unlocking it.\n\nA THIRD SURVIVING SIDE EFFECT I MISSED, and it is the sharpest finding here.\n`WorkGiver_DoBill.ShouldSkip` walks every `PotentialBillGiver` on the map asking\n`BillStack.AnyShouldDoNow` -\u003e `Bill_Production.ShouldDoNow`, which **writes the\nscribed `paused` flag** on three paths. `makingFor` does not close it.\n\nThe asymmetry is the point: **spec 2.4's `bills` verb BANS `ShouldDoNow`\noutright** via WorldSafe Class A precisely so it can read a bill stack without\nwriting it. `orders` cannot refuse — the call sits inside vanilla's `JobOnThing`,\nbelow our reach. So the same accessor is forbidden to one verb and unavoidable\nin another, and the honest answer is disclosure rather than suppression.\n\nCORRECTION TO MY OWN BODY: `nextTickToSearchForIngredients` is NOT scribed\n(`RimWorld/Bill.cs ExposeData` does not touch it). I never claimed it was, but I\nput it next to the correct `nextJobID`-is-scribed point, which invites the\nmisread. The harm is runtime colony-wide suppression, not save corruption.\n\nRESTORE, NOT NULL — a deliberate deviation from vanilla with a reason. Vanilla\nnulls unconditionally because it is the ONLY setter and its pass cannot re-enter\nitself; we are a second setter. `prevMakingFor` is null on every real path\n(verbs drain in `GameComponentUpdate`, the float menu builds during OnGUI, so\nthey cannot interleave), so this is identical to vanilla everywhere except the\none case where nulling would clear a value we did not set.\n\nNEW HAZARD CLASS — `PawnSafe` gained **Class G**, and it is worth reading beyond\nthis issue: *the hazard is not always a getter.* `orders` cited its widget,\ncopied the provider's clause-for-clause logic, called no banned accessor, and\nstill mutated — because what it omitted lived in the provider's CALLER. Every\nprior hazard in this project was \"do not read X\". This one is \"you reproduced\nthe callee and not its caller's setup\".\n\nACCEPTANCE HAS A WRINKLE THE ORCHESTRATOR MUST KNOW. AutoRimmer did not expose\n`nextTickToSearchForIngredients`, so the worker added\n`next_ingredient_search_tick` / `ingredient_search_cooldown` to `bills` —\ninstrument and fix landed in the same branch, so **the \"moves by 500-600\"\nmeasurement cannot be run against main's shipped DLL.**\n`accept/32b9e01-orders-makingfor.md` therefore offers four phases, and phase M\nis the one to run if only one: on `main`, `orders` against an ingredient-starved\nbill returns NO entry at all; on this branch it returns a `blocked` entry\ncarrying the game's own `MissingMaterials` string. That needs no instrumentation\nand no repro build.\n\nPhase D is worth running regardless of the fix: it shows a single `orders` call\nflipping a bill's scribed `paused` false-\u003etrue on EITHER build. That is the\nmeasurement behind \"asking is not free\".\n\nThe fixture table names four ways to get a green run for the wrong reason\n(`skill_min` default 4 short-circuits the ingredient search; a satisfied bill; a\nbutcherable corpse in range; a drafted pawn). Read it before believing a pass.","files":null}]}